---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// The colophon: a short, plain page behind the nav link for anyone who wants
// the explanation the instrument itself never gives out loud. The instrument
// is still the site (index.html is where a visitor lands, per
// spec/crit-4.test.ts) — this is a second page about it, not a substitute for
// playing it.
---

<Layout
  title="Handpan — about"
  description="How the browser handpan works: live synthesis, the tuning arc, sympathetic resonance, the phrase memory, the keyboard map, and damping."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>About the handpan</h1>
  </header>

  <main class="about">
    <p>
      Nine tone fields — a central ding and eight around it — synthesised live from oscillators
      that never stop running. Nothing on the instrument is a recording; every note is a
      fundamental, its octave, a compound fifth and some inharmonic shimmer, shaped by how hard
      and where you land. Because every field is tuned to the same scale, there is no wrong
      note to strike — any combination of them is intentional.
    </p>

    <dl>
      <dt>Strike</dt>
      <dd>
        Loudness comes from how fast your hand was moving before it touched the field, not from
        how hard you press — a mouse click and a firm poke report the same pressure, but not the
        same speed. Where you land matters too: the centre rings warm and plain, the rim brings
        out the overtones.
      </dd>

      <dt>Resonance</dt>
      <dd>
        Striking one field quietly excites the neighbours it shares a harmonic relationship
        with — a fifth, an octave — the way one part of a steel shell answers another. It is
        audible as a faint undertone and visible as a second, dimmer glow arriving a beat after
        the one you made.
      </dd>

      <dt>Memory</dt>
      <dd>
        Play three or four notes and stop. After a short pause the shell answers on its own —
        quieter, shifted to a related note, fading out over a few repeats. Its own strikes glow
        a cool blue rather than the warm amber of yours, so the two are never mistaken for each
        other by ear or by eye.
      </dd>

      <dt>Tuning</dt>
      <dd>
        A groove runs low across the rim, in front of the ding. Drag its bead — or tab to it and
        use the arrow keys — and the whole scale glides from D Kurd toward a Celtic minor
        tuning while any notes already ringing slide along with it, live.
      </dd>

      <dt>Keys</dt>
      <dd>
        <kbd>b</kbd> <kbd>v</kbd> <kbd>n</kbd> <kbd>f</kbd> <kbd>j</kbd> <kbd>r</kbd>
        <kbd>u</kbd> <kbd>4</kbd> <kbd>7</kbd> climb the nine fields in scale order, alternating
        hands the way the pan's own layout does. Every field is also a plain button, so Tab and
        Enter reach all nine without the key map.
      </dd>

      <dt>Damping</dt>
      <dd>
        Hold a field down past a beat — with a hand, a finger, or a key — and it mutes like a
        palm laid on real steel. A quick tap always just rings.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </main>

  <footer class="colophon">
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="./index.html">Instrument</a>
    </nav>
  </footer>
</Layout>
